    ("Maximum Likelihood Estimation", "log-likelihood maximised", "The log is increasing, so it has the same maximiser as the likelihood, but a sum of logs is easier to differentiate than a product and avoids numerical underflow from multiplying many small probabilities."),
    ("Fine-Tuning", "is fine-tuning?", "Pre-training provides general features from a large dataset; continuing the training on a small task dataset reuses them, so the new task needs far less data and compute than training from scratch."),
    ("Fine-Tuning", "small learning rate", "Large updates would move the weights far from the pre-trained solution and destroy what it learned (catastrophic forgetting); small steps nudge it toward the new task."),
    ("Embedding", "property should an embedding space have", "An embedding is useful because geometry encodes meaning: nearby vectors stand for similar items, so distances and directions can be used for search, clustering and generalisation."),
    ("Embedding", "embedding table used", "A lookup replaces a sparse one-hot vector times a matrix: the id picks a row directly, and because that row is a trainable parameter the vectors are adjusted by gradient descent like any other weight."),
    ("Markov Decision Process", "Markov property say", "If the state summarises everything relevant from the past, the agent can decide from it alone, which makes the problem tractable with dynamic programming instead of needing the full history."),
    ("Markov Decision Process", "elements define", "Together these specify what the agent can observe and do (states, actions), how the world responds (transitions), what is good (rewards) and how future reward is weighed (discount); with them the optimal behaviour is well defined."),
    ("Markov Decision Process", "discount factor", "Each step into the future multiplies the reward by gamma, so a small gamma makes rewards beyond a few steps nearly worthless while a gamma near one makes distant rewards almost as important as immediate ones."),
    ("Bellman Equation", "express", "A state's value splits into what you get now and what you can expect afterwards; because the second part is the value of the next state, the definition is recursive and can be solved by iteration."),
    ("Policy Gradient", "optimise directly", "Instead of learning values and deriving actions, the policy's own parameters are adjusted in the direction that raises the expected return; this works for continuous actions and stochastic policies."),
    ("Policy Gradient", "subtract a baseline", "Subtracting a quantity that does not depend on the action leaves the expected gradient unchanged but removes the common offset in returns, so individual estimates fluctuate less and learning is steadier."),
    ("Value Function", "does a value function estimate", "It summarises the future for a given policy as one number per state (or state-action), which lets the agent compare situations without simulating every continuation."),
    ("Q-Learning", "on-policy or off-policy", "The update target uses the maximum over next actions, not the action the agent actually takes, so it learns about the greedy policy while behaving differently, for instance exploring."),
