    ("Probability", "Bayes' rule", "It follows from writing the joint probability two ways, P(A, B) = P(A | B) P(B) = P(B | A) P(A); it lets you reverse a conditional, turning how likely the data is under a hypothesis into how likely the hypothesis is given the data."),
    ("Probability", "expected value", "Weighting each possible value by how likely it is gives the centre of the distribution; by the law of large numbers the average of many independent draws approaches it."),
    ("Calculus", "derivative of a function at a point", "The ratio of the output change to a tiny input change settles to a limit, the slope of the tangent; it tells which way, and how fast, the function moves, which is what gradient-based learning uses."),
