    ("Information Theory", "mutual information", "It compares the joint distribution with the product of the marginals; if the variables are independent the two are equal and the information is zero, and the more one tells about the other the larger it is."),
    ("Optimization", "move against the gradient", "A small step along the negative gradient lowers the function to first order, because the gradient is the direction of fastest increase; repeating the step walks downhill."),
    ("Optimization", "learning rate is too large", "The gradient describes the function only near the current point; a long step can land on the opposite slope and higher up, and repeating that makes the iterates oscillate or diverge."),
    ("Optimization", "convex function", "A convex function lies below the chord between any two points, so it has no separate valleys; wherever descent stops, it is at the global minimum, which gives the guarantee that non-convex losses lack."),
